  },
  {
   "k": "4x682d",
   "en": "Main article: Construction"
  },
  {
   "k": "1pvovg4",
   "en": "Construction can be divided into three categories: state buildings, shared buildings and province buildings."
  },
  {
   "k": "35fg7j",
   "en": "State buildings"
  },
  {
   "k": "4phtlx",
   "en": "State buildings are state-wide and every state can reach the same maximum level for each building."
  },
  {
   "k": "1ts0ged",
   "en": "Infrastructure, airbases, anti-air and radar stations belong to this category."
  },
  {
   "k": "1gmkfgd",
   "en": "Shared buildings"
  },
  {
   "k": "11yiwio",
   "en": "Shared buildings are also state-wide, but the amount that can be built is limited to the number of free slots. Once all slots are occupied, no more shared buildings can be built."
  },
  {
   "k": "aph4qm",
   "en": "Factories, dockyards, synthetic refineries, fuel silos, rocket sites, and nuclear reactors belong to this category"
  },
  {
   "k": "rqds14",
   "en": "Province buildings"
  },
  {
   "k": "10klkfi",
   "en": "Province buildings are built per-province, their maximum levels depend on the type of building."
  },
  {
   "k": "n4cvzx",
   "en": "Naval bases, forts and supply hubs belong to this category."
  },
  {
   "k": "15g8w42",
   "en": "Resources"
  },
  {
   "k": "g7m8xh",
   "en": "Main article: Production#Resources"
  },
  {
   "k": "1okp0by",
   "en": "Resources for production are generated at the state level. The majority controller will have a certain amount of the resources of a state added to their resource pool depending on their occupation policy."
  },
  {
   "k": "1s4ispe",
   "en": "Population"
  },
  {
   "k": "10na2hr",
   "en": "The population in a state is tracked by five numbers."
  },
  {
   "k": "1jv71ck",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧ is the population the state starts with; it is shown on the state's info panel. Monthly population growth is based on it and the total never declines. The game's duration usually spans less than a generation, so all inhabitants that could enter military service are already born at the start of the game.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "1fdzfbs",
   "en": "total population"
  },
  {
   "k": "c3utpz",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧ is available for the controlling country to use in military service. Recruitable population gets reduced as ⟦1⟧ is used in the military, not just after taking casualties.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "1jqu7tg",
   "en": "recruitable population"
  },
  {
   "k": "19uembk",
   "en": "manpower"
  },
  {
   "k": "scl48j",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧ is a fraction of the total population that limits the recruitable population. For how the fraction is calculated, see ⟦1⟧.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "1vn427w",
   "en": "recruitable population cap"
  },
  {
   "k": "176kq0g",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧ is the portion of the population that is not available for military service.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "1rn6irr",
   "en": "civilian population"
  },
  {
   "k": "1qqw5ay",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧ is a fraction of the total population that limits the civilian population. It is the total population minus the recruitable population cap.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "1wjfk9n",
   "en": "civilian population cap"
  },
  {
   "k": "1qkiqsr",
   "en": "Changes in the effective recruitable population ratio can cause one of the two population counts to overflow. In this case population is transferred to the other population type. For example, if a country raises its recruitment law, the civilian population cap will go down over time (according to the mobilization speed), which causes some civilian population to be transferred to recruitable population. Conversely, if a state is captured by a country that does not have a core on it, the recruitable ratio will be massively reduced and might cause recruitable population to transfer back to civilian population."
  },
  {
   "k": "1cr7p3a",
   "en": "Growth"
  },
  {
   "k": "vawbly",
   "en": "The state's total population grows by ⟦0⟧ at the start of every month. The increase in population is distributed between recruitable and the civilian population by the recruitable ratio. Both increases are always at least one person respectively.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
